All,

The contents of this email are confidential and not to be shared outside th=
e company until further notice.  If you have a commercial situation where y=
ou might want to use this call me for consideration.  This confidentiality =
applies to Info Blurt 49 also.  The original communication on this project.

The results came back on the testing of the surface of the pipe at the fail=
ure location.  See commentary below and attached graphs.  The pipe is extre=
mely oxidized.  This was determined to be the cause of failure as explained=
 to me by the President of UGSI.

Chlorine Gas was the disinfection system.  The operating pressure at this f=
ailure location was 80 psi.  This is a DR9 pipe.  This stress in the pipe i=
s much lower than the typical golf irrigation system sees (120 psi pressure=
 in DR13.5 pipe).  Have not gotten the service temperature but I would expe=
ct that it was no more than 80o.

Sunbelt courses using reclaimed water and municipal water are in for a roug=
h time in a few more years.

John




From: Tom Marti
Sent: Friday, February 26, 2016 5:42 AM
To: Frank Firsching
Cc: Robert Tatum; Ray Dudley; Marty Scanlan
Subject: Initial FTIR results

This is an update on the 30" DR 9 HDPE section returned from the Florida.  =
I had sent ESI a small section cut several inches away from the fracture ar=
ea.  I then sent ESI  the actual fracture surfaces to test as I had found d=
uring the initial inspection that the surface features varied around the in=
ternal diameter.  As Dale reports below the CI for the fracture area is ext=
remely high.  Usually a 0.2 or higher is  considered oxidized.  These test =
show a CI of over 0.7.

I will be discussing with ESI today.  Does the client expect a formal repor=
t back on these tests?  If so, let me know and I can get one started before=
 we pass the results on to the client.

From: Dale Edwards [mailto:dbedwards@engsys.com]
Sent: Thursday, February 25, 2016 5:48 PM
To: Tom Marti
Subject: Initial FTIR results

Tom,

I shaved a film from the inner surface along the crack at one end of one of=
 the samples for ATR-FTIR.  It shows extreme oxidation in this area, with a=
 carbonyl index of 0.7375.  The other side of the film, approximately 10 mi=
ls into the surface has a carbonyl index of 0,0537. I have attached pdf's o=
f the two ATR-FTIR runs with peak measurements.

This pipe is very oxidized.  I will try and get a microtome samples for mic=
ro-FTIR done tomorrow, but obviously this pipe in the fracture area is extr=
emely oxidized and is oxidized likely for several mils into the surface.

Dale


Please note that our firm's e-mail addresses have been updated to a new dom=
